<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The app's privacy manifest.

  **What is declared, and why each line is here.**

  *No tracking.* There is no advertising SDK, no analytics, no attribution and no third party of
  any kind in the bundle, so there is nothing to declare and no domain to list. `NSPrivacyTracking`
  false is the whole of it.

  *User defaults, two reasons.* `CA92.1` covers the store the app keeps for itself: every
  preference in `PreferenceKeys`, the session history, the macro list and the SSH hosts. `1C8F.1`
  covers the App Group container, which exists for one reason and is described on the entitlement
  itself: the home screen widget runs in its own container and cannot read the app's defaults, so
  the Mac it opens is written to a store both targets can see.

  *File timestamps, for the crash log.* `C617.1` covers reading the modification date of a file in
  the app's own container. That is the whole of its use: a crash is written by a signal handler that
  cannot ask the clock what time it is, because formatting a date inside one is not a safe thing to
  do, so the file's own modification time is the crash time and it is read back on the next launch.
  Nothing outside the app's own container is stat'd, and nothing about the timestamps leaves the
  device, because the crash reports themselves do not.

  *A device identifier, and this is the line worth arguing about.* The app sends the relay an
  identifier it made for itself, kept in user defaults, and the relay writes it beside the seconds
  that identifier has spent relayed, in an append-only file. That is data leaving the device and
  outliving the request that carried it, which is Apple's definition of collection, so it is
  declared rather than left out: `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID`, not linked to anybody and
  not used to track anything, for app functionality only.

  It is a random UUID and not a device identifier in the sense the category usually means. It is
  not the advertising identifier, it is not the vendor identifier, it exists nowhere outside this
  app and the relay, and it identifies an installation rather than a person or a machine. Declaring
  it is the conservative reading, and the conservative reading of this file is the right one: a
  manifest is a statement to Apple, and understating one is the failure that costs.

  *What is deliberately not declared.* Purchases. The app sends the relay the receipt Apple signed,
  because that is how a subscriber is metered as a subscriber, and the relay verifies it against
  Apple's root and keeps it for the life of the connection and nowhere else. It is never written to
  a log, a database or a file. Data processed in memory to answer the request in front of it is not
  collected.

  **This file and the App Store Connect questionnaire have to agree, and only one of them is in
  this repository.** If the answer typed into App Store Connect says "Data Not Collected" while
  this says otherwise, that is a review finding. Both are downstream of the same decision.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
